{align:justify|On Sungshan Avenue in bustling Taipei there was a roast-duck shop famed far and wide, called the "High-Class Delicacy." Business boomed, customers streamed in and out, the premises were vast and elegantly appointed, and the young male and female staff all wore white Western uniforms. The head chef wore a spotless white hat and gloves at all times, advertising utter hygiene.}
{align:justify|From dawn to night the staff spun like tops, receiving and serving diners. As for the owner, surnamed Thai, past fifty, round and plump in his Western suit, he sat beaming behind the cash register and money counter, a glittering gold watch on his wrist, grinning so wide his mouth would not close...}
{align:justify|Before the shop stood the retail counter; behind it lay the slaughterhouse and the duck-roasting ovens — guaranteeing duck meat always fresh, fragrant, and delicious, drawing diners back again and again. So trade flourished ever more.}
{align:justify|In recent years branches opened everywhere; the owner kept expanding his killing trade, and his circle of feasting companions grew — inevitably including patrons of wine-houses, dance halls, gold shops, casinos, and the like... This was the season of his utter self-indulgence, when he knew every climax of human pleasure.}
{align:justify|Each year when spring came and every household shopped for the New Year, with friends and kin exchanging gifts, the High-Class shop's business surged like a rising tide. Regulars wanting an oven-fresh roast duck had to queue three to five hours. Staff bustled till past midnight; the shop hired extra temporary hands yet stayed dizzyingly busy without rest — until the whole family was mobilized, all hands thrown into buying and selling.}
{align:justify|One day, like any other, the shop was buzzing and roaring with trade when suddenly, in a single instant, a tremendous duck-squawk rang out, drowning every other noise and seizing everyone's attention. Yet all who witnessed it stood frozen, rooted to the spot. In that moment they could only stare, wide-eyed, at old owner Thai sprawled on the floor, shaped exactly like a duck, his mouth ceaselessly crying "quack-quack" — not only like a duck's, but ten thousand times louder. The crowd pressed around, buzzing with talk, when a stout woman suddenly screamed:}
{align:justify|"Good heavens! This old man killed so many ducks he's getting his retribution! How dreadful! I'll never dare eat duck again!"}
{align:justify|Only then did everyone come to their senses and, as if of one mind, swarm out of the shop like a broken beehive.}
{align:justify|The lady manager hastily called doctors, but however skilled, no physician could stop that uncanny quacking. He cried so for three days and three nights, until, strength spent and voice gone, his eyes bulged wide, blood gushed from all seven orifices, and he breathed his last in uttermost torment.}
{align:justify|From that day the "High-Class Delicacy" sign was taken down, branches everywhere closed, and the Thai clan hid all trace of itself — none knows where they moved.}
